our series earth 365 focuses on issues  that affect our planet year round one of  the most anticipated voices at the U.N  climate Summit currently underway in  Egypt doesn't belong to a politician or  a scientist it belongs to a 25 year old  Ugandan climate activist named Vanessa  nakante senior National and  environmental correspondent Ben Tracy  recently caught up with her as the world  prepared to focus attention on her home  continent of Africa  we don't need any more words we need  real action  Vanessa nakate benefits from the clarity  of Youth the 25 year old climate  activist is tired of politicians saying  a lot about the climate crisis and then  doing too little about it the climate  crisis is impacting people right now and  yet we are still you know holding  conferences we are still holding Summits  and yet people continue to suffer and so  your point is stop talking start doing  exactly  scientists warn that the impacts of  climate change are accelerating and  efforts to curb Planet warming pollution  are not nearly enough necate whose  influential activism has landed her on  the cover of Time Magazine now finds  herself in some ways representing a  continent of 1.4 billion people that is  uniquely vulnerable we've seen some of  the worst impacts of the climate crisis  you know across Africa it is  heartbreaking and frustrating because  you didn't cause this crisis but then  you are suffering the impacts Africa  accounts for the smallest share of  Planet warming greenhouse gas emissions  a fraction of what China the U.S and the  European Union emit but climate change  is punishing the African continent all  the same rising sea levels record  flooding and extreme heat waves and  drought are intensifying famine and  displacing entire communities  communities are struggling to find 4  good to it they're struggling to find  water because the entire place is dry we  talk here a lot about can we afford  electric cars is there enough wind and  solar but what you're saying is this is  much more acute life and death struggles  there are people that are suffering  severely and they don't need an electric  vehicle they need food they need water  nakate started a youth climate movement  in her native Uganda in 2019 by  protesting on the street we are  unstoppable she says she was inspired by  Swedish climate activist Greta tunberg  who waged her own climate strike when  she was just 15. what is it do you think  that young people understand about  climate change that a lot of adults  don't seem to I think young people  understand the science of climate change  and what is really happening I think  they understand the agency of the  problem  [Applause]  but it's important to know that young  people can't do this alone people say  you know the future is in your hands but  the people with the money to transform  communities they hold you know the power  to effect change in Africa we've really  had crazy floods in 2020 nakante spoke  truth to the powerful at the world  economic Forum in Davos Switzerland she  then posed for a photograph with four  other young climate activists all of  them white including Greta tunberg but  when the Associated Press released the  photo that was printed around the world  nakate was cropped out that was one of  the most you know heartbreaking moments  in my activism the world is so cruel in  an emotional 10-minute video she posted  to Twitter necate said it wasn't just  her face that was being erased does that  mean that I have no value as an African  activist or the people from Africa don't  have any value at all  one of the key things that I emphasize  during the press conference was the need  to listen to every voice so it was  heartbreaking for me to see that picture  and not see myself that it certainly  reinforced the message you were trying  to deliver in a really unfortunate way  and it really showed how those that are  on the front lines are not on the front  pages the AP later apologized and  nakante has used that experience to fuel  her fight for climate Justice and make  sure African faces and voices are seen  and heard there are always moments of  frustration when we met up at the  Brooklyn Botanical Garden she told us  she still remains hopeful the world will  make progress on combating climate  change I have to find a place of hope  because it is in that hope that I get  the strength to continue fighting in the  meantime she says the rich nations of  the world the so-called Global North  need to make good on their promise to  provide 100 billion a year to poor  countries like Uganda to help them adapt  to climate change we need to move from  promises to real action I promise  gives hope for a while  but when it's broken it brings  disappointment we need real action and  real action is possible for CBS mornings  I'm Ben Tracy in New York  well thank you Ben for introducing many  people to Vanessa I first saw on the  cover of Time Magazine but as painful  and insulting is that deliberate  decision was to cut her out of the  picture it now has drawn more attention  to her and what she's doing and it's the  order yeah and it motivated her yes yes  yes I liked what what Ben said she  benefits from the clarity of Youth and  she's over conferences and Summits now  people let's do something yeah thank you  very much
 

